```java
class Point {
    int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (!(o instanceof Point p)) return false;
        return x == p.x && y == p.y;
    } // hashCode NOT overridden — still uses Object's identity hash
}

Set<Point> seen = new HashSet<>();
seen.add(new Point(1, 2));
seen.contains(new Point(1, 2)); // false! different objects, different hash codes
```

The two `Point(1, 2)` objects are `equals()`, but `contains()` still returns `false` — the second one hashes to a different bucket, so `HashSet` never even calls `equals()` on the first.

```java
class Point {
    final int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (!(o instanceof Point p)) return false;
        return x == p.x && y == p.y;
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(x, y); }
}
```

Now both methods agree on exactly the same fields (`x` and `y`), and both fields are `final` so they can never change after construction — the standard, safe pattern for a value object used as a hash key.

```java
class Point {
    int x, y; // NOT final — mutable

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) { /* compares x, y */ return true; }
    @Override
    public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(x, y); }
}

Point p = new Point(1, 2);
Set<Point> seen = new HashSet<>();
seen.add(p);
p.x = 99; // mutate AFTER inserting
seen.contains(p); // false — p now hashes to a different bucket than it was stored in
```

`equals`/`hashCode` are correct in isolation — the bug is that `x` changed *after* insertion, so the object is now looking in the wrong bucket for itself. This is why hash keys should be immutable.
